{"name":"Honey Oat Bread","hex":"#faeed9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sweet, viscous, gold-colored fluid produced from plant nectar by bees, and often consumed by humans.","A variety of this substance."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Involving or resembling honey.","Of a pale yellow to brownish-yellow color, like most types of honey."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey"},{"term":"oat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Widely cultivated cereal grass, typically Avena sativa.","Any of the numerous species, varieties, or cultivars of any of several similar grain plants in genus Avena."]},{"part":"Prepositional phrase","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of OAT."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oat"},{"term":"bread","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A foodstuff made by baking dough made from cereals.","A foodstuff made by baking dough made from cereals."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To coat with breadcrumbs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bread"}],"entries":[{"title":"Finnish bread","blocks":[{"text":"Bread is a staple food of Finland. It is served with almost every meal and many different types are produced domestically."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Ruisleip%C3%A4-limppu_reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_reissumies_hapankorppu-1.JPG/330px-Ruisleip%C3%A4-limppu_reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_reissumies_hapankorppu-1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnish_bread","wikidata":"Q5450828"},{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"},{"title":"Oat","blocks":[{"text":"The oat, sometimes called the common oat, is a species of cereal grass (Avena) grown for fodder and for its seed, which is known by the same name. Oats appear to have been domesticated as a secondary crop, as their seeds resembled those of other cereals closely enough for them to be included by early cultivators. Oats tolerate cold winters less well than cereals such as wheat, barley, and rye, but need less summer heat and more rain, making them important in areas such as Northwest Europe that have cool, wet summers. They can tolerate low-nutrient and acid soils. Oats grow thickly and vigorously, allowing them to outcompete many weeds, and compared to other cereals are relatively free from diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/AvenaSativa3.jpg/330px-AvenaSativa3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oat","wikidata":"Q12104"},{"title":"Bread","blocks":[{"text":"Bread is a baked food product made from water, flour, and often yeast. It is a staple food across the world, particularly in Europe and the Middle East. Throughout recorded history and around the world, it has been an important part of many cultures' diets. It is one of the oldest human-made foods, having been of significance since the dawn of agriculture, and plays an essential role in both religious rituals and secular culture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Korb_mit_Br%C3%B6tchen.JPG/330px-Korb_mit_Br%C3%B6tchen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bread","wikidata":"Q7802"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Finnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg/500px-Finnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Finnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg/1280px-Finnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Finnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg/500px-Finnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sourdough starter mix, the base of most Finnish rye bread","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFinnish_sour_dough_starter_mix_-_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Mischbrot-1.jpg/500px-Mischbrot-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Mischbrot-1.jpg/1280px-Mischbrot-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Mischbrot-1.jpg/500px-Mischbrot-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Limppu","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMischbrot-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg/500px-Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_view_from_above.jpg/1280px-Reik%C3%A4leip%C3%A4_vie